WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:03.376
This... is a market simulator

00:00:03.376 --> 00:00:05.811
that&nbsp;we're going to build in this video.

00:00:05.811 --> 00:00:08.700
And we're using it to see and understand

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.760
trade-based manipulation in action.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.048
Unlike fake news,

00:00:13.048 --> 00:00:16.233
this manipulation exploits specific trading patterns

00:00:16.233 --> 00:00:20.678
seeming legal at first glance&nbsp;
as it involves no blatant lies.

00:00:20.678 --> 00:00:25.000
And this isn't just a story about&nbsp;
smaller, less-regulated markets.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.097
The foreign exchange market, the&nbsp;
largest financial market in the world,

00:00:29.097 --> 00:00:32.349
was traditionally believed&nbsp;
to be too big to manipulate,

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:35.141
which turned out to be a costly mistake.

00:00:35.141 --> 00:00:37.485
Discovered in 2013,

00:00:37.485 --> 00:00:40.893
a handful of traders orchestrated
what became known as

00:00:40.893 --> 00:00:45.841
'The FOREX Case', siphoning away hundreds of millions for years.

00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:48.531
Thankfully, we're not completely helpless.

00:00:48.531 --> 00:00:51.590
With some math and financial forensic&nbsp;
tools,

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.911
we can spot the manipulators.

00:00:54.911 --> 00:00:58.495
And here is our protagonist, who&nbsp;
wants to take a chance in the market

00:00:58.495 --> 00:01:01.379
by trading shares of a rocket company.

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:06.171
And, just like many of us, they all have a hard time making good predictions.

00:01:06.171 --> 00:01:09.064
So they buy and sell randomly.

00:01:09.064 --> 00:01:13.182
And for now, our friend is lucky&nbsp;
… winning most of the time.

00:01:13.182 --> 00:01:15.530
Not every trader can be that lucky, though!

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.550
But they still believe in the fairness of the market,

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.419
trusting that everyone has an equal chance.

00:01:21.419 --> 00:01:25.347
Then, out of nowhere, some new players show up.

00:01:25.347 --> 00:01:27.820
And they're not just winning sometimes;

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.960
they're consistently beating the odds.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.818
How are they doing it?

00:01:32.818 --> 00:01:36.117
At first, it seems like these bad actors&nbsp;
might just be

00:01:36.117 --> 00:01:38.773
better at predicting the future.

00:01:38.773 --> 00:01:40.304
But here's the twist:

00:01:40.304 --> 00:01:42.630
in a&nbsp;
market driven by randomness,

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.960
there shouldn't be a reliable&nbsp;
way to predict the future.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.523
So… how are they pulling it off?

00:01:48.523 --> 00:01:50.809
And even more interesting,

00:01:50.809 --> 00:01:53.783
the market still looks mostly random.

00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:57.748
The bad actors blend in almost perfectly.

00:01:57.748 --> 00:02:00.057
So, what I want to build with you today

00:02:00.057 --> 00:02:03.710
is a way to measure and expose this manipulation.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:07.074
Let's break this down and see how this fraud works

00:02:07.074 --> 00:02:09.786
because market manipulation isn't just

00:02:09.786 --> 00:02:12.019
about numbers moving on a screen.

00:02:12.549 --> 00:02:15.028
This video is sponsored by Brilliant.

00:02:15.028 --> 00:02:17.738
More at the end of the show.

00:02:17.738 --> 00:02:18.937
Quick disclaimer:

00:02:18.937 --> 00:02:23.101
I'm not a financial advisor, and this&nbsp;
video is for educational purposes only.

00:02:23.101 --> 00:02:24.605
Always do your own research.

00:02:25.857 --> 00:02:30.061
The price movement we just saw, comes from a real simulation I built.

00:02:30.061 --> 00:02:32.183
And here's how it works:

00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:35.832
Every day, people come to&nbsp;
the market to trade shares.

00:02:35.832 --> 00:02:40.812
Not everyone is ready to trade, though
some folks are just observing the market.

00:02:40.812 --> 00:02:43.410
The others are placing orders.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.843
Take this buyer, for example.

00:02:45.843 --> 00:02:50.047
This person wants to buy 70 shares at 53&nbsp;
dollars each

00:02:50.047 --> 00:02:52.116
but wouldn't mind a lower price

00:02:52.116 --> 00:02:56.135
that's the maximum price this&nbsp;
person is willing to pay.

00:02:56.135 --> 00:03:00.330
And, as I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;
they trade completely randomly.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:05.634
So, their order prices could be higher&nbsp;
or lower yesterday's market price.

00:03:05.634 --> 00:03:10.193
Think of these buy orders as coming&nbsp;
from a probability distribution.

00:03:10.193 --> 00:03:15.311
And this randomness in the simulation&nbsp;
is a great proxy to mirror a fair market

00:03:15.311 --> 00:03:18.213
one where everyone has an equal chance.

00:03:18.213 --> 00:03:22.896
Here, no one knows what's going&nbsp;
to happen, and that's fair.

00:03:22.896 --> 00:03:27.334
Now, the shares must come from&nbsp;
someone namely the sellers.

00:03:27.334 --> 00:03:31.638
This person might want to sell 50 shares at 45&nbsp;
dollars each,

00:03:31.638 --> 00:03:33.405
but wouldn't mind a higher price

00:03:33.405 --> 00:03:37.358
that's the minimum price at which&nbsp;
this person is willing to sell.

00:03:37.358 --> 00:03:42.804
And these sell orders also come from&nbsp;
their own probability distribution.

00:03:42.804 --> 00:03:46.465
Now, let's set a single market price for the day.

00:03:46.465 --> 00:03:49.390
Some people are very satisfied with this price,

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:52.154
while others may skip trading altogether.

00:03:52.154 --> 00:03:54.835
So, how is the price actually set?

00:03:54.835 --> 00:03:57.974
One way is to maximize total satisfaction;

00:03:57.974 --> 00:04:01.219
the extent to which orders are over-fulfilled.

00:04:01.219 --> 00:04:02.540
To calculate it:

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.191
all the buy&nbsp;
and sell orders are lined up.

00:04:05.191 --> 00:04:06.723
And the ideal price,

00:04:06.723 --> 00:04:11.493
the one that&nbsp;
brings the most satisfaction, lies somewhere in this overlap.

00:04:11.493 --> 00:04:13.460
Here, it's not a unique price.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.621
Any price within this range achieves&nbsp;
the same maximum satisfaction.

00:04:17.621 --> 00:04:21.234
But picking the middle&nbsp;
ground is a practical choice.

00:04:21.234 --> 00:04:25.056
And this price range also&nbsp;
determines the day's trading volume;

00:04:25.056 --> 00:04:27.990
the number of shares that change hands.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.356
So, if we set a different single price,

00:04:30.356 --> 00:04:34.541
one side would be more willing to trade
while the other would be less willing;

00:04:34.541 --> 00:04:37.143
reducing overall agreement.

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:39.629
As a result, fewer shares are traded,

00:04:39.629 --> 00:04:43.060
since both sides must be satisfied for a deal to happen.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:46.483
This method is called auction pricing.

00:04:46.483 --> 00:04:50.401
In real-world markets, it's often&nbsp;
used at the start of the trading day

00:04:50.401 --> 00:04:54.555
to match all the overnight orders&nbsp;
to a single, opening price.

00:04:54.555 --> 00:04:56.904
During a continuous trading day,

00:04:56.904 --> 00:04:59.510
orders are&nbsp;
often instantly matched against each other

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:02.380
leading to multiple prices&nbsp;
in the short time frame.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:04.705
But that's a story for another time.

00:05:04.705 --> 00:05:08.214
Here, I assume a single trading moment at each day

00:05:08.214 --> 00:05:10.857
using auction pricing.

00:05:10.857 --> 00:05:14.639
And this is what the full market simulation looks like.

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:18.175
We start with setting up the order price distributions.

00:05:18.175 --> 00:05:22.247
In reality, buyers and sellers might have different expectations,

00:05:22.247 --> 00:05:24.571
leading to different distributions.

00:05:24.571 --> 00:05:28.289
But for today's video, I don't&nbsp;
really need this degree of freedom,

00:05:28.289 --> 00:05:30.981
so I'll use the same bell curve for both,

00:05:30.981 --> 00:05:33.691
centered around yesterday's market price.

00:05:33.691 --> 00:05:37.131
This represents the idea that&nbsp;
people expect their orders to be

00:05:37.131 --> 00:05:39.450
filled within a familiar price range.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:41.297
After all, it worked yesterday,

00:05:41.297 --> 00:05:44.296
so they hope it will work again today.

00:05:44.296 --> 00:05:46.812
From these order curves, the market determines the

00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:49.502
current day's price and trading volume,

00:05:49.502 --> 00:05:52.072
which we track over time in a graph.

00:05:52.072 --> 00:05:55.188
And with that, the simulation loop is complete.

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:59.842
Today's market price becomes the starting point for tomorrow's price distribution

00:05:59.842 --> 00:06:01.706
and the cycle continues.

00:06:01.706 --> 00:06:04.716
New order curves generate new prices and volumes,

00:06:04.716 --> 00:06:07.177
and the market evolves step by step.

00:06:07.177 --> 00:06:09.836
As a result, the market moves randomly,

00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:13.165
making it impossible to predict in a useful way.

00:06:13.165 --> 00:06:17.949
Since each day's price distribution is centered&nbsp;
around the previous day's market price,

00:06:17.949 --> 00:06:21.997
there's no way to know if&nbsp;
prices will rise or fall.

00:06:21.997 --> 00:06:24.227
Now that we have the simulation&nbsp;
running,

00:06:24.227 --> 00:06:26.516
let's see what it tells us.

00:06:26.516 --> 00:06:30.520
One obvious fact is that markets&nbsp;
don't operate in isolation.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:32.786
External events like breaking news

00:06:32.786 --> 00:06:35.472
are constantly reshaping expectations.

00:06:35.472 --> 00:06:40.088
So, these distributions shift&nbsp;
as people's expectations evolve.

00:06:40.088 --> 00:06:42.764
For example, when good news arrives,

00:06:42.764 --> 00:06:45.426
a company's true value may be higher than

00:06:45.426 --> 00:06:47.916
what's reflected in its current stock price.

00:06:47.916 --> 00:06:51.161
And as more and more traders&nbsp;
become aware of the news,

00:06:51.161 --> 00:06:55.880
they anticipate that competition&nbsp;
will eventually drive prices higher.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.326
To simplify our simulation, these&nbsp;
random traders skip

00:06:59.326 --> 00:07:04.043
the waiting game and instantly adjust their&nbsp;
expectations to the new price level.

00:07:04.043 --> 00:07:08.307
And just like that, the market&nbsp;
price adjusts almost immediately.

00:07:08.307 --> 00:07:11.655
And as rational beings, we tend to think ahead.

00:07:11.655 --> 00:07:15.553
If we believe a company will continue delivering good news,

00:07:15.553 --> 00:07:18.256
we might anticipate future price jumps.

00:07:18.256 --> 00:07:21.002
And to benefit from these future&nbsp;
price jumps,

00:07:21.002 --> 00:07:24.966
buyers must act quickly, before others bid the price up&nbsp;
further,

00:07:24.966 --> 00:07:26.909
or they risk missing out.

00:07:26.909 --> 00:07:29.725
This again creates competition,

00:07:29.725 --> 00:07:32.810
driving&nbsp;
prices even higher almost instantly.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:36.692
In other words, it's not just today's&nbsp;
news that drives stock prices,

00:07:36.692 --> 00:07:42.740
but expectations about all future news&nbsp;
also get baked into the price immediately.

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:44.731
But here comes the next twist:

00:07:44.731 --> 00:07:47.662
to make&nbsp;
this market really hard to manipulate,

00:07:47.662 --> 00:07:50.791
we feed no additional information to the traders.

00:07:50.791 --> 00:07:54.186
They ignore all news and rumors about the future.

00:07:54.186 --> 00:07:56.755
So, really, no one can manipulate

00:07:56.755 --> 00:07:59.093
these traders with faulty information.

00:07:59.093 --> 00:08:02.116
They're as unpredictable as it gets.

00:08:02.116 --> 00:08:06.870
Ok, so far, we've created a fair market where randomness rules.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:09.325
Buyers and sellers act unpredictably,

00:08:09.325 --> 00:08:12.875
and prices reflect their&nbsp;
combined random expectations.

00:08:12.875 --> 00:08:15.394
But what if there are there&nbsp;
invisible forces subtly

00:08:15.394 --> 00:08:18.415
influencing the direction of these random movements?

00:08:18.415 --> 00:08:21.494
And could this cause certain price levels to be avoided,

00:08:21.494 --> 00:08:24.108
creating soft barriers in the market?

00:08:24.108 --> 00:08:26.943
Well, let's see what the simulation says.

00:08:27.882 --> 00:08:31.690
Imagine running simulations for 500 days.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:34.372
It's surprisingly easy to spot&nbsp;
patterns

00:08:34.372 --> 00:08:35.976
that look like order.

00:08:35.976 --> 00:08:39.305
But of course: in this scenario, it's all random.

00:08:39.305 --> 00:08:41.786
Yet, not all patterns are meaningless.

00:08:41.786 --> 00:08:44.702
And recognizing these patterns helps&nbsp;
us understand

00:08:44.703 --> 00:08:48.635
how markets function and where limits emerge naturally.

00:08:48.635 --> 00:08:51.679
For example, running the simulation a million times

00:08:51.679 --> 00:08:54.058
shows which price levels are rare.

00:08:54.058 --> 00:08:57.138
This symmetry won't tell you exactly how to trade,

00:08:57.138 --> 00:09:01.574
but it does offer insights into&nbsp;
the overall shape of the market.

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:04.808
Let's do this for two hypothetical markets:

00:09:04.808 --> 00:09:07.320
In the first market, traders place orders

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.569
based on the price distribution
and a uniform volume distribution.

00:09:11.569 --> 00:09:15.946
So, the volumes could range anywhere&nbsp;
from 0 to the maximum trade volume,

00:09:15.946 --> 00:09:18.406
each with an equal probability.

00:09:18.406 --> 00:09:23.671
Most importantly, they have unlimited wealth to back all their trades.

00:09:23.671 --> 00:09:28.231
This means they can always afford whatever volume they want to order at any price,

00:09:28.231 --> 00:09:31.269
buying and selling completely randomly.

00:09:31.269 --> 00:09:34.079
As a result, the orders the traders can offer

00:09:34.079 --> 00:09:36.858
are independent of the absolute price level.

00:09:36.858 --> 00:09:40.207
And this means that the typical daily price&nbsp;
changes,

00:09:40.207 --> 00:09:42.042
derived from the order curves,

00:09:42.042 --> 00:09:45.834
are also essentially independent&nbsp;
of the absolute price level.

00:09:45.834 --> 00:09:48.556
Each day acts like an independent&nbsp;
experiment,

00:09:48.556 --> 00:09:53.128
detached from the past, and then stacked on top of the previous days.

00:09:53.128 --> 00:09:57.293
The prices in this market follow what's known as a "Gaussian random walk,"

00:09:57.293 --> 00:10:00.495
with an expected price change of zero.

00:10:00.495 --> 00:10:03.776
And even if the real market price suddenly spikes,

00:10:03.776 --> 00:10:06.464
we've learned that each trading day operates

00:10:06.464 --> 00:10:08.557
independently from the previous ones.

00:10:08.557 --> 00:10:11.575
And so the forecast shape remains unchanged.

00:10:11.575 --> 00:10:15.182
There's no external force pulling&nbsp;
the price back down again.

00:10:15.182 --> 00:10:18.280
You're essentially running the same&nbsp;
prediction again,

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:20.264
just at a new price level.

00:10:20.264 --> 00:10:23.198
In such a random walk, it's likely&nbsp;
for prices

00:10:23.198 --> 00:10:27.199
to eventually reach arbitrarily high or low values over&nbsp;
time;

00:10:27.199 --> 00:10:30.223
here on a logarithmic price scale.

00:10:30.223 --> 00:10:33.389
Now, contrast this with the second market,

00:10:33.389 --> 00:10:36.192
where traders are limited by their wealth.

00:10:36.192 --> 00:10:39.811
Here, the forecast shape differs significantly.

00:10:39.811 --> 00:10:40.805
Why?

00:10:40.805 --> 00:10:43.189
It comes down to the trading rules.

00:10:43.189 --> 00:10:46.725
In this market, traders can't&nbsp;
borrow money to buy shares,

00:10:46.725 --> 00:10:50.489
so their buy volumes are&nbsp;
limited by what they can afford.

00:10:50.489 --> 00:10:52.231
When prices get too high,

00:10:52.231 --> 00:10:55.061
their&nbsp;
ability to buy decreases sharply.

00:10:55.061 --> 00:10:58.989
They simply get fewer shares for&nbsp;
the money they're willing to spend.

00:10:58.989 --> 00:11:01.480
Selling, however, is less restricted.

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:06.365
They can place sell orders for any portion of their holdings at virtually any price,

00:11:06.365 --> 00:11:09.089
regardless of whether anyone buys.

00:11:09.089 --> 00:11:12.381
These sell orders could be wishful thinking.

00:11:12.381 --> 00:11:15.000
This introduces an asymmetry:

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:20.057
as prices rise, the volume of buy&nbsp;
orders shrinks due to the lack of money,

00:11:20.057 --> 00:11:23.035
while sell orders remain here unaffected.

00:11:23.035 --> 00:11:25.775
If prices drift too far from the center,

00:11:25.775 --> 00:11:30.040
this asymmetry creates a natural pull,
nudging them back.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:34.040
We can see this more clearly when&nbsp;
we examine the theoretical limit.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:36.595
Here, we assume an infinite number of&nbsp;
traders

00:11:36.595 --> 00:11:38.924
placing buy and sell orders.

00:11:38.924 --> 00:11:42.858
This blends individual actions&nbsp;
seamlessly into the overall system,

00:11:42.858 --> 00:11:47.393
which gives smoother curves and&nbsp;
reveals the market dynamics better.

00:11:47.393 --> 00:11:51.091
Now, comparing this to the Gaussian&nbsp;
random walk of the first market,

00:11:51.091 --> 00:11:55.756
where the shape of the order curves remained&nbsp;
independent of the absolute price level,

00:11:55.756 --> 00:11:59.644
we clearly see the additional&nbsp;
price nudge in the second market.

00:11:59.644 --> 00:12:02.075
Prices still fluctuate randomly,

00:12:02.075 --> 00:12:05.505
but now&nbsp;
they orbit a sort of gravitational center.

00:12:05.505 --> 00:12:09.370
Over time, these fluctuations settle into a band.

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:12.096
In theory, prices could rise indefinitely.

00:12:12.096 --> 00:12:16.359
But it becomes increasingly&nbsp;
unlikely, given the order asymmetry.

00:12:16.359 --> 00:12:18.904
This is a fascinating observation!

00:12:18.904 --> 00:12:22.131
The rules governing how our people can trade

00:12:22.131 --> 00:12:26.490
effectively create practical,
soft limits on price movements.

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:29.187
At least when no one is talking about it.

00:12:29.187 --> 00:12:34.324
In reality, any publicly known price limit is quickly exploited and disappears.

00:12:34.324 --> 00:12:36.950
But assuming for a moment this limit holds,

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:38.892
then this it, what it means:

00:12:38.892 --> 00:12:42.134
When our traders keep adding new money to their trades,

00:12:42.134 --> 00:12:44.755
they are injecting new funds into the market,

00:12:44.755 --> 00:12:48.414
and a statistically significant&nbsp;
trend channel forms.

00:12:48.414 --> 00:12:50.000
And think about this:

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:54.564
At no point does the simulation instruct the market to form channels.

00:12:54.564 --> 00:12:57.234
This pattern emerges naturally as a result

00:12:57.234 --> 00:13:00.610
of the market rules
and the traders' order behavior.

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:04.549
And this trend is different from the&nbsp;
trends in a Gaussian random walk.

00:13:04.549 --> 00:13:09.010
Those trends were just coincidental&nbsp;
and lacked real significance.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:12.229
Even if we add a drift to a Gaussian random walk,

00:13:12.229 --> 00:13:15.000
it won't produce this kind of band channel.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.821
That's worth investigating.

00:13:16.821 --> 00:13:18.013
But not today.

00:13:18.013 --> 00:13:22.212
Because there are some more useful price&nbsp;
movement patterns I want to show you.

00:13:22.212 --> 00:13:26.212
For example, increasing the number of traded&nbsp;
shares, the volume,

00:13:26.212 --> 00:13:28.239
makes prices less volatile.

00:13:28.239 --> 00:13:29.226
Why?

00:13:29.226 --> 00:13:32.094
Well, few orders make choppy order curves

00:13:32.094 --> 00:13:35.049
which generate on average bigger price jumps.

00:13:35.049 --> 00:13:39.406
More orders make smoother curves&nbsp;
and average out to smaller jumps.

00:13:39.406 --> 00:13:42.154
Here, I've rescaled the volume in the diagram

00:13:42.154 --> 00:13:45.094
to better fit the order curves within the screen.

00:13:45.094 --> 00:13:46.779
So, with a lot of orders,

00:13:46.779 --> 00:13:49.961
say, a&nbsp;
million, the price barely wobbles.

00:13:49.961 --> 00:13:53.708
Sure, prices can still shift&nbsp;
if expectations surge suddenly,

00:13:53.708 --> 00:13:56.353
but they don't fluctuate wildly.

00:13:56.353 --> 00:13:58.449
Here's what this means in real life:

00:13:58.449 --> 00:14:00.684
When you see a massive price jump,

00:14:00.684 --> 00:14:03.488
it&nbsp;
doesn't necessarily signify major news.

00:14:03.488 --> 00:14:06.329
It could be fewer people trading.

00:14:06.329 --> 00:14:09.084
Fewer trades make bigger jumps.

00:14:09.084 --> 00:14:11.144
And if you want to put these jumps into context,

00:14:11.144 --> 00:14:15.746
you can draw a band showing what's "normal" at various volume levels.

00:14:15.746 --> 00:14:21.082
I share the code in my tutorials so you can see how these factors influence market movements.

00:14:21.082 --> 00:14:25.741
So, these patterns highlight how trading behavior shapes randomness.

00:14:25.741 --> 00:14:28.135
And in the toughest market imaginable,

00:14:28.135 --> 00:14:29.604
the Gaussian random walk,

00:14:29.604 --> 00:14:32.497
you cannot make predictions that can be exploited

00:14:32.497 --> 00:14:35.465
which ties back to our initial question.

00:14:35.465 --> 00:14:41.085
How can you reliably win in a market where everyone else acts completely at random?

00:14:41.085 --> 00:14:43.740
Imagine yourself in that situation.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:48.183
To win every single time, you'd need&nbsp;
to know something about the future.

00:14:48.183 --> 00:14:50.136
But there's our problem:

00:14:50.136 --> 00:14:52.733
you can't predict&nbsp;
what everyone else will do.

00:14:52.733 --> 00:14:54.612
It's all random.

00:14:54.612 --> 00:14:59.121
So, if predicting others&nbsp;
isn't an option, what's left?

00:14:59.121 --> 00:15:03.001
The only variable that can shape&nbsp;
the future to your advantage...

00:15:03.001 --> 00:15:04.866
is you …

00:15:04.866 --> 00:15:08.331
Winning isn't about predicting the market anymore,

00:15:08.331 --> 00:15:10.324
it's about taking control.

00:15:10.324 --> 00:15:14.205
And control means more than just reacting faster,

00:15:14.205 --> 00:15:18.944
it's about dictating prices and shaping&nbsp;
how others perceive market trends.

00:15:18.944 --> 00:15:23.858
And this hints at the level of&nbsp;
influence required to make it happen.

00:15:23.858 --> 00:15:26.820
It might seem like an obvious realization,

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:30.294
but I wanted to point it out because it's so important.

00:15:30.294 --> 00:15:34.360
In the next section, we'll explore the mechanics of this control

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:39.263
and see how deliberate actions can distort a random market.

00:15:40.009 --> 00:15:44.270
So, what would it take to nudge a market's price in your favor?

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:47.050
For instance, let's say you bought some shares,

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:49.878
and now you want to artificially&nbsp;
increase the market price.

00:15:49.878 --> 00:15:51.820
The first step is simple:

00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:53.510
I enter the market and say,

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:57.327
"I'm buying 600 shares at 55 dollars each!"

00:15:57.327 --> 00:16:02.337
Remember, the buyer curve shows how much people are willing to pay at various prices.

00:16:02.337 --> 00:16:05.332
So, when we slot my order into this buyer curve,

00:16:05.332 --> 00:16:07.596
the market price takes a step up.

00:16:07.596 --> 00:16:11.592
In other words, my order adds more demand at a higher price point,

00:16:11.592 --> 00:16:13.807
justifying a higher market price.

00:16:13.807 --> 00:16:17.526
By adjusting my order, tweaking the price or&nbsp;
the volume,

00:16:17.526 --> 00:16:19.486
I can influence the market price.

00:16:19.486 --> 00:16:23.343
All it takes is placing enough&nbsp;
volume at higher price levels.

00:16:23.343 --> 00:16:27.370
But there's a catch: this&nbsp;
'strategy' comes with a cost.

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:30.064
I can't just talk about buying these shares.

00:16:30.064 --> 00:16:33.018
I actually have to buy them to cause a price shift.

00:16:33.018 --> 00:16:35.722
And that's incredibly expensive.

00:16:35.722 --> 00:16:39.470
My action moved the price, but at full cost.

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:42.366
Still, I shouldn't expect anyone's sympathy.

00:16:42.366 --> 00:16:45.109
This tactic is usually considered illegal

00:16:45.109 --> 00:16:48.290
since&nbsp;
my intention was to mislead other traders.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:51.218
And if the goal is to&nbsp;
exploit other traders anyway,

00:16:51.218 --> 00:16:53.990
there are ways to achieve&nbsp;
more impact for the cost,

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:57.265
and we don't even need to&nbsp;
spread faulty news for that.

00:16:57.987 --> 00:17:02.419
So, how can we manipulate the market&nbsp;
price without footing the bill?

00:17:02.419 --> 00:17:05.462
At first glance, if we place a big buy order,

00:17:05.462 --> 00:17:07.493
we'd end up paying the seller

00:17:07.493 --> 00:17:10.917
600 shares times whatever the market price will be.

00:17:10.917 --> 00:17:14.656
To break even, we need to get that money back somehow.

00:17:14.656 --> 00:17:17.437
The trick is to sell the same number of shares

00:17:17.437 --> 00:17:19.414
right back,
to ourselves.

00:17:19.414 --> 00:17:22.116
This practice is called self-trading.

00:17:22.116 --> 00:17:24.025
But here's the interesting part:

00:17:24.025 --> 00:17:27.940
we're not just moving shares from&nbsp;
one pocket to another in secret.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:30.487
Instead, we're doing it publicly:

00:17:30.487 --> 00:17:34.284
From the left pocket, through the exchange, into the right pocket,

00:17:34.284 --> 00:17:37.643
making it look like legitimate market activity.

00:17:37.643 --> 00:17:41.419
By itself, self-trading doesn't&nbsp;
magically change the market price.

00:17:41.419 --> 00:17:44.127
It simply shifts supply and demand.

00:17:44.127 --> 00:17:46.205
The point is perception.

00:17:46.205 --> 00:17:50.336
Faking higher volume creates&nbsp;
the illusion of a liquid market.

00:17:50.336 --> 00:17:52.535
Which is great because, in real markets,

00:17:52.535 --> 00:17:55.432
traders often see high volume as a signal that

00:17:55.432 --> 00:17:58.800
something big is happening,
like breaking news.

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:00.377
But remember our catch:

00:18:00.377 --> 00:18:04.472
in our simulation,
this fake volume doesn't influence anyone.

00:18:04.472 --> 00:18:08.676
Our traders act randomly,&nbsp;
ignoring these signals entirely.

00:18:08.676 --> 00:18:12.641
And that's what makes this problem so challenging.

00:18:12.641 --> 00:18:14.919
If perception alone won't work,

00:18:14.919 --> 00:18:17.760
we need to directly influence the market price;

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:21.869
a strange concept when most traders take prices as given.

00:18:21.869 --> 00:18:24.410
These are price takers.

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:25.990
To manipulate the market,

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:28.867
we&nbsp;
have to think like price setters.

00:18:28.867 --> 00:18:30.641
And here's how it works:

00:18:31.002 --> 00:18:35.245
Imagine a small stock where daily volume is usually low.

00:18:35.245 --> 00:18:39.047
Placing a buy order of 600 shares at $55

00:18:39.047 --> 00:18:42.775
and a sell order of 600 shares at $55

00:18:42.775 --> 00:18:45.920
creates the illusion of high activity.

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:47.686
As we increase the volume,

00:18:47.686 --> 00:18:51.070
we pay&nbsp;
more but still lack full control.

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:53.798
But when the buy and sell orders&nbsp;
overlap,

00:18:53.798 --> 00:18:55.608
we reach a tipping point.

00:18:55.608 --> 00:18:58.659
Every trade within that range is effectively

00:18:58.659 --> 00:19:01.513
self-ordering at a zero price difference.

00:19:01.513 --> 00:19:04.224
This enforces the intersection of the curves

00:19:04.224 --> 00:19:07.104
which gives us control of the market price.

00:19:07.104 --> 00:19:11.649
And once we have control, we can&nbsp;
set any price and volume we want,

00:19:11.649 --> 00:19:16.071
pushing it high enough to&nbsp;
overcompensate for any initial costs.

00:19:16.071 --> 00:19:20.140
We've successfully hijacked&nbsp;
the market's pricing mechanism.

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:23.182
This is called monopoly power in a stock market,

00:19:23.182 --> 00:19:26.547
where 'buying the whole market' lets you dictate prices.

00:19:26.547 --> 00:19:29.547
It's similar to how a company with a product monopoly

00:19:29.547 --> 00:19:32.597
can manipulate supply to raise costs.

00:19:32.597 --> 00:19:36.654
In real markets, this tactic&nbsp;
doesn't just distort the price.

00:19:36.654 --> 00:19:40.777
It can also trick traders into&nbsp;
believing it's genuine activity.

00:19:40.777 --> 00:19:44.183
It's particularly easy in smaller, less regulated&nbsp;
markets,

00:19:44.183 --> 00:19:46.606
where the initial cost is lower.

00:19:46.606 --> 00:19:48.260
So, as you can see:

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:53.252
With trade-based manipulation, one can distort signals like price and volume

00:19:53.252 --> 00:19:56.529
and trick traders into making poor decisions.

00:19:56.529 --> 00:20:02.154
So, our friend should watch for one unusually&nbsp;
large buy and sell order at nearly the same price.

00:20:02.154 --> 00:20:05.578
That could be an attempt at manipulation.

00:20:06.156 --> 00:20:09.685
Alright, so our friend here is running low on funds,

00:20:09.685 --> 00:20:11.877
just like the rest of the 'good' traders.

00:20:11.877 --> 00:20:14.452
It's time to step in and help them out

00:20:14.452 --> 00:20:16.886
by cracking their opponent's moves.

00:20:16.886 --> 00:20:20.383
We're going to walk through one&nbsp;
complete manipulation cycle,

00:20:20.383 --> 00:20:22.149
to really understand it.

00:20:22.149 --> 00:20:24.988
And then we'll figure out how to blend these moves

00:20:24.988 --> 00:20:28.640
seamlessly
into the noise of the crowd.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:32.600
Let's take a closer look at&nbsp;
how the whole scheme works,

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:35.720
here in a market that follows&nbsp;
a Gaussian random walk.

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:37.802
And to better see what's happening,

00:20:37.802 --> 00:20:41.453
we track the manipulator's money, shares, and the relative wealth

00:20:41.453 --> 00:20:43.632
compared to the rest of the market.

00:20:43.632 --> 00:20:47.671
That's the total money you hold,&nbsp;
plus the market value of your shares,

00:20:47.671 --> 00:20:50.330
compared to what the other traders have.

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:54.383
So, first, the scheme starts with buying as many shares as possible

00:20:54.383 --> 00:20:56.631
without drawing too much attention.

00:20:56.631 --> 00:20:58.917
The goal is to stay under the radar

00:20:58.917 --> 00:21:01.469
while&nbsp;
gradually building up your position.

00:21:01.469 --> 00:21:03.217
So, in the graphs, we see

00:21:03.217 --> 00:21:06.395
money&nbsp;
slowly being converted into shares.

00:21:06.395 --> 00:21:09.037
Then comes the price manipulation.

00:21:09.037 --> 00:21:11.650
This involves using self-trading, where you

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:14.021
partly buy and sell shares to yourself,

00:21:14.021 --> 00:21:17.173
to gain control and nudge the price upward.

00:21:17.173 --> 00:21:20.927
Here, I took a rather obvious&nbsp;
approach to demonstrate the mechanics.

00:21:20.927 --> 00:21:23.087
But you can make it less noticeable,

00:21:23.087 --> 00:21:25.840
nudging the price up by a small percentage.

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:27.374
It won't work every day.

00:21:27.374 --> 00:21:32.014
Some days, you might not have full control, or the market could move unpredictably.

00:21:32.014 --> 00:21:33.815
Well, that's part of the gamble.

00:21:33.815 --> 00:21:37.813
As a manipulator, I wouldn't say&nbsp;
you are in a position to complain.

00:21:37.813 --> 00:21:42.221
During this phase, you're also buying&nbsp;
more shares to maintain control.

00:21:42.221 --> 00:21:46.388
Finally, once the price has been&nbsp;
inflated enough, it's time to sell.

00:21:46.388 --> 00:21:49.424
The goal here is to offload your shares quietly,

00:21:49.424 --> 00:21:54.086
cashing in on the inflated price&nbsp;
without causing too much disruption.

00:21:54.086 --> 00:21:58.755
By the end of the cycle, you've gone from&nbsp;
having no shares and a million dollars,

00:21:58.755 --> 00:22:02.463
to holding no shares again, but now with&nbsp;
a larger amount of

00:22:02.463 --> 00:22:05.211
1.7 million dollars.

00:22:05.211 --> 00:22:07.253
You can see in the relative&nbsp;
wealth graph

00:22:07.253 --> 00:22:12.129
that we've managed to take a significant slice&nbsp;
from the other random traders.

00:22:12.129 --> 00:22:15.712
This is a form of the classic&nbsp;
"pump-and-dump" scheme.

00:22:15.712 --> 00:22:20.274
What makes the method here stand out&nbsp;
is that it's entirely trade-based.

00:22:20.274 --> 00:22:21.793
We don't issue fake news.

00:22:21.793 --> 00:22:25.489
We just&nbsp;
control the price with self-trading.

00:22:25.489 --> 00:22:27.319
To show it works on average,

00:22:27.319 --> 00:22:31.169
let's imagine the same situation without manipulation.

00:22:31.169 --> 00:22:35.177
You buy shares for the same amount of money over the same period

00:22:35.177 --> 00:22:37.950
but don't artificially inflate the price.

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:41.038
Many simulations show that the non-manipulated&nbsp;
version

00:22:41.038 --> 00:22:44.499
consistently underperforms compared to the manipulated one,

00:22:44.499 --> 00:22:47.133
proving the scheme's effectiveness.

00:22:47.133 --> 00:22:51.269
What's particularly troubling is that&nbsp;
even when people aren't trading randomly,

00:22:51.269 --> 00:22:54.774
like in the final phase when&nbsp;
they start selling alongside you,

00:22:54.774 --> 00:22:59.455
it's still possible to predict their&nbsp;
behavior and factor it into your plan.

00:22:59.455 --> 00:23:03.108
In fact, if the scheme works&nbsp;
in a completely random market,

00:23:03.108 --> 00:23:07.837
it works even better when traders&nbsp;
follow predictable patterns.

00:23:07.837 --> 00:23:11.711
Now, where does the manipulator's&nbsp;
profit actually come from?

00:23:11.711 --> 00:23:15.796
There's one driving factor that&nbsp;
makes this whole scheme possible:

00:23:15.796 --> 00:23:21.085
the assumption that tomorrow's price&nbsp;
is likely to be close to today's price.

00:23:21.085 --> 00:23:24.418
If traders didn't rely so&nbsp;
much on recent price trends,

00:23:24.418 --> 00:23:27.867
meaning they wouldn't simply&nbsp;
accept the inflated price level,

00:23:27.867 --> 00:23:31.225
this kind of manipulation wouldn't be possible.

00:23:31.225 --> 00:23:35.000
You might think that basing trades&nbsp;
more on fundamental factors,

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.142
like a company's performance,&nbsp;
sounds like the rational approach,

00:23:38.142 --> 00:23:39.772
and you'd be right.

00:23:39.772 --> 00:23:42.584
But it only works if everyone does it.

00:23:42.584 --> 00:23:46.897
Markets are inherently driven by&nbsp;
participants outbidding one another,

00:23:46.897 --> 00:23:50.529
fully aware that they are&nbsp;
trading at irrational prices.

00:23:50.529 --> 00:23:53.819
This, in itself, is also a&nbsp;
fundamental factor:

00:23:53.819 --> 00:23:55.692
the human factor.

00:23:55.692 --> 00:23:59.385
That said, our self-trades&nbsp;
here are still easy to spot.

00:23:59.385 --> 00:24:02.229
And exchanges could simply ignore these&nbsp;
orders,

00:24:02.229 --> 00:24:04.440
or regulators might investigate them.

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:08.747
So, let's figure out how manipulators&nbsp;
manage to blend into the crowd.

00:24:08.747 --> 00:24:12.266
Because, you can't investigate,&nbsp;
what you don't know about.

00:24:13.061 --> 00:24:15.980
By now, you've probably spotted the trick:

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:18.604
it's not a crowd of people moving the market.

00:24:18.604 --> 00:24:21.620
It's one person pulling the&nbsp;
strings behind the scenes.

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:26.654
The idea is to scatter coordinated&nbsp;
self-trading orders across the market,

00:24:26.654 --> 00:24:29.149
making them look like harmless activity,

00:24:29.149 --> 00:24:30.918
except they're not.

00:24:30.918 --> 00:24:33.181
These trades still carry enough weight

00:24:33.181 --> 00:24:36.183
to&nbsp;
nudge the price where the manipulator wants it.

00:24:36.183 --> 00:24:39.020
If we spread these orders out more smoothly,

00:24:39.020 --> 00:24:42.421
the manipulation becomes much harder to detect.

00:24:42.421 --> 00:24:45.516
There are still some unusual&nbsp;
dents in the order curves,

00:24:45.516 --> 00:24:49.006
but now they're more subtle&nbsp;
and could go undetected.

00:24:49.006 --> 00:24:52.952
Now, how could manipulation&nbsp;
be concealed even better?

00:24:52.952 --> 00:24:55.443
First, we need to learn how to quantify it.

00:24:55.443 --> 00:24:57.605
There are several ways to go about it:

00:24:57.605 --> 00:25:02.003
The idea is to identify irregularities&nbsp;
in the order curve shape.

00:25:02.003 --> 00:25:05.502
To achieve this, we can&nbsp;
exemplarily standardize the curves

00:25:05.502 --> 00:25:08.557
to fit a 0–100% volume range

00:25:08.557 --> 00:25:12.097
and the&nbsp;
expected un-manipulated price range.

00:25:12.097 --> 00:25:15.510
Then we run multiple&nbsp;
simulations without manipulation

00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:18.976
to establish a reference&nbsp;
for normal market behavior.

00:25:18.976 --> 00:25:22.434
This creates a band of order curves,
helping quantify

00:25:22.434 --> 00:25:24.926
what counts as "unusual" behavior.

00:25:24.926 --> 00:25:27.538
And as we have seen for price setting,

00:25:27.538 --> 00:25:29.677
manipulation has a tell.

00:25:29.677 --> 00:25:33.816
You'd expect unusual behavior in both order curves.

00:25:33.816 --> 00:25:37.539
There are several ways to assign numbers to this unusual behavior

00:25:37.539 --> 00:25:40.317
to estimate how much manipulation is happening.

00:25:40.317 --> 00:25:41.962
If you're interested,

00:25:41.962 --> 00:25:46.471
my&nbsp;
coding tutorials dive into building the simulation and running these tests.

00:25:46.471 --> 00:25:48.299
Also, on a related note,

00:25:48.299 --> 00:25:51.125
we are looking into an intriguing connection&nbsp;
between

00:25:51.125 --> 00:25:56.359
wealth distribution in markets and the statistical mechanics of an ideal gas.

00:25:56.359 --> 00:26:01.323
Both systems have conserved quantities like total money, shares, or energy.

00:26:01.323 --> 00:26:04.644
And as traders or particles continuously interact,

00:26:04.644 --> 00:26:08.706
they naturally settle into&nbsp;
equilibrium distributions.

00:26:08.706 --> 00:26:13.017
In that sense, you can loosely&nbsp;
think of a market as having a 'money temperature,'

00:26:13.017 --> 00:26:16.563
which offers&nbsp;
an interesting perspective on markets.

00:26:16.563 --> 00:26:18.858
But for now, let's go back and see how

00:26:18.858 --> 00:26:22.834
the amount&nbsp;
of manipulation varies across different scenarios.

00:26:22.834 --> 00:26:25.238
Here are three distinct price jumps,

00:26:25.238 --> 00:26:28.126
but&nbsp;
not all of them are caused by manipulation.

00:26:28.126 --> 00:26:30.320
For example, this jump right here?

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:34.795
That's a genuine market reaction to positive news about the company.

00:26:34.795 --> 00:26:39.893
We can simulate this with our traders reacting to what is called a sentiment curve.

00:26:39.893 --> 00:26:42.403
Think of the sentiment curve as a signal

00:26:42.403 --> 00:26:46.267
reflecting the market's collective&nbsp;
belief about a company's future.

00:26:46.267 --> 00:26:48.887
Positive news can shift the curve upward,

00:26:48.887 --> 00:26:51.679
representing increased confidence of investors.

00:26:51.679 --> 00:26:54.541
With fresh money injected into the market,

00:26:54.541 --> 00:26:58.416
both the trading volume and price drive upward.

00:26:58.416 --> 00:27:02.744
Compare that to a random price&nbsp;
jump caused by low trading volume.

00:27:02.744 --> 00:27:05.161
Here, the sentiment curve stays flat.

00:27:05.161 --> 00:27:07.385
Nothing external has changed.

00:27:07.385 --> 00:27:10.228
However, if we had inflated&nbsp;
the volume with

00:27:10.228 --> 00:27:12.941
self-trading without actually price setting,

00:27:12.941 --> 00:27:15.982
it would have looked more like news than noise.

00:27:15.982 --> 00:27:17.605
At least on the surface.

00:27:17.605 --> 00:27:22.145
A price jump accompanied by increased volume seems natural for good news.

00:27:22.145 --> 00:27:27.736
But self-trading leaves a trace, making it detectable in the manipulation graph.

00:27:27.736 --> 00:27:28.908
One important note:

00:27:28.908 --> 00:27:31.972
this graph isn't proof of manipulation.

00:27:31.972 --> 00:27:34.611
Patterns like these occur naturally.

00:27:34.611 --> 00:27:38.797
Eventually, it's about identifying&nbsp;
traders with repeated suspicious behavior.

00:27:38.797 --> 00:27:40.978
And only when more instances stack up

00:27:40.978 --> 00:27:43.771
does it trigger further investigation.

00:27:43.771 --> 00:27:47.045
Now, what happens when we go for price-setting?

00:27:47.045 --> 00:27:49.051
That's the pump-and-dump method.

00:27:49.051 --> 00:27:51.057
The sentiment stays flat.

00:27:51.057 --> 00:27:53.685
There's no real news driving the price.

00:27:53.685 --> 00:27:56.873
So, all the activity is fake.

00:27:56.873 --> 00:27:59.786
And here's the thing about fraud detection:

00:27:59.786 --> 00:28:01.988
it's a constant cat-and-mouse game.

00:28:01.988 --> 00:28:04.032
Let me show you an example.

